-- Worked example 1: overlapping 101 detector, Moore version
type st_t is (S0, S1, S10, S101);   -- S101 means "101 seen"
process (clk) begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then
    case st is
      when S0   => if x = '1' then st <= S1;   end if;
      when S1   => if x = '0' then st <= S10;  end if;
      when S10  => if x = '1' then st <= S101; else st <= S0;  end if;
      when S101 => if x = '1' then st <= S1;   else st <= S10; end if;
    end case;
  end if;
end process;
z_moore <= '1' when st = S101 else '0';
-- Mealy version: states M0, M1, M10 and z_mealy <= '1' when st = M10 and x = '1'

-- cnt : unsigned(15 downto 0); N = 50_000 gives a 1 kHz tick from 50 MHz
process (clk)
begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then
    tick <= '0';                    -- default: low
    if cnt = N - 1 then
      cnt  <= (others => '0');
      tick <= '1';                  -- high for one clock every N clocks
    else
      cnt <= cnt + 1;
    end if;
  end if;
end process;

questions.questionText:
Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.numeric_std. a is std_logic_vector(2 downto 0) = "101" and y is std_logic_vector(7 downto 0). What is y?

process (all)
begin
  y <= (others => '0');
  y(to_integer(unsigned(a))) <= '1';
end process;

Assume VHDL-2008. d is std_logic_vector(3 downto 0) and holds "0101". What is y?

process (all)
begin
  case? d is
    when "1---" => y <= "11";
    when "01--" => y <= "10";
    when "001-" => y <= "01";
    when others => y <= "00";
  end case?;
end process;

Assume ieee.numeric_std. a and b are signed(3 downto 0) with a = "1001" and b = "0111":

gt <= '1' when a > b else '0';
eq <= '1' when a = b else '0';
lt <= '1' when a < b else '0';

What are gt, eq and lt?

The 4-bit ripple-carry adder below has a = "1011", b = "0110" and cin = '1'. What are s and cout?

c(0) <= cin;
gen : for i in 0 to 3 generate
  s(i)   <= a(i) xor b(i) xor c(i);
  c(i+1) <= (a(i) and b(i)) or (c(i) and (a(i) xor b(i)));
end generate;
cout <= c(4);

Which outputs of this combinational process are implemented with latches?

process (all)
begin
  if sel = '1' then
    x <= a;
    y <= b;
  else
    x <= b;
  end if;
end process;

The process below has rst missing from its sensitivity list. The clock has a 10 ns period, with rising edges at 5, 15 and 25 ns and falling edges at 10 and 20 ns. d = '1', q became '1' at the 5 ns edge, and rst goes to '1' at 7 ns and stays there. When does q become '0' in simulation?

process (clk)
begin
  if rst = '1' then
    q <= '0';
  elsif rising_edge(clk) then
    q <= d;
  end if;
end process;

How many flip-flops does synthesis infer for this code, where cnt is unsigned(3 downto 0) and msb_d is std_logic?

process (clk) begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then
    cnt   <= cnt + 1;
    msb_d <= cnt(3);
  end if;
end process;
y <= cnt(3) xor msb_d;

din changes between two clock edges. Version V of a clocked process uses variables and version S uses signals:

-- V
if rising_edge(clk) then
  v1 := din;  v2 := v1;  q <= v2;
end if;
-- S
if rising_edge(clk) then
  s1 <= din;  s2 <= s1;  q <= s2;
end if;

How many rising edges later does q show the new din, and how many flip-flops are inferred?

cnt is an ieee.numeric_std unsigned(2 downto 0) with initial value 0, and the process below is clocked at 60 MHz. How often does tick pulse high?

process (clk)
begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then
    if cnt = 0 then
      cnt  <= to_unsigned(5, 3);
      tick <= '1';
    else
      cnt  <= cnt - 1;
      tick <= '0';
    end if;
  end if;
end process;

cnt is unsigned(3 downto 0):

if rising_edge(clk) then
  if load = '1' then
    cnt <= unsigned(d);
  elsif up = '1' then
    cnt <= cnt + 1;
  else
    cnt <= cnt - 1;
  end if;
end if;

At six successive edges (load, up, d) are (1, 0, "1110"), (0, 1, –), (0, 1, –), (1, 1, "0011"), (0, 0, –) and (0, 0, –). What is cnt after the sixth edge?

ones and tens are unsigned(3 downto 0), both starting at 0:

if rising_edge(clk) then
  if ones = 9 then
    ones <= (others => '0');
    if tens = 9 then tens <= (others => '0'); else tens <= tens + 1; end if;
  else
    ones <= ones + 1;
  end if;
end if;

What do tens and ones hold after 123 rising edges?

sig comes from a flip-flop on the same clock, so it changes just after rising edges; it goes to '1' and stays '1' for 5 clock periods:

process (clk) begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then sig_d <= sig; end if;
end process;
pulse <= sig and not sig_d;

For how long is pulse '1'?

type state_t is (IDLE, LOAD, RUN, DONE); and the FSM below, inside if rising_edge(clk) then, starts in IDLE:

case state is
  when IDLE => if start = '1' then state <= LOAD; end if;
  when LOAD => state <= RUN;
  when RUN  => if stop = '1' then state <= DONE; end if;
  when DONE => state <= IDLE;
end case;

At five successive rising edges (start, stop) = (1, 0), (0, 1), (0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0). What is the state after the fifth edge?

A traffic-light FSM starts in GREEN with t = 0, where t is integer range 0 to 3:

if rising_edge(clk) then
  case state is
    when GREEN  => if t = 2 then state <= YELLOW; t <= 0; else t <= t + 1; end if;
    when YELLOW => state <= RED; t <= 0;
    when RED    => if t = 1 then state <= GREEN; t <= 0; else t <= t + 1; end if;
  end case;
end if;

What are the state and t after 8 rising edges?
